   "body": "**mac/mac-mbp**: one more case from the Fable review of #1385 (finding 4). It happens at `dev-swift` too, and #1385 does not fix it. #1385 changes only the app's take-over; the engine-side echo described here stays.\n\n**A copy dated ahead freezes the stored credentials.** While the synchronizable copy's `credentialsAt` is later than this device's clock, every write-back of this device loses the merge (`AccountSyncMerge.newer`, a tie keeps the remote: `AccountSyncMerge.swift:67-71`). The merge then writes the copy's account into the device-only store (`AccountSync.swift:316-321`).\n- Reviewer's control probe: after a valid check, the stored `md_user` keeps the copy's expiry (1 day), not the refreshed one (+30 days).\n- The next cold launch rebuilds the session from that frozen copy. Once its cookies lapse, the account is logged out at every launch.\n- A re-login on this device (`.login`, stamped with this device's `now`) also loses the merge while the copy stays ahead.\n- ADR 0013 accepts clock skew only for `enabledAt` and `deletedAt`, not for `credentialsAt`.\n\n**Fix direction** (to decide here, alongside A1): the A1 fix (an expiry-only refresh does not move `credentialsAt`) does not cover this case by itself, because a real re-login here still loses. Options:\n- (a) Clamp: a local change is stamped `max(now, remote.credentialsAt + step)`, the same way `enabledAt` is (`AccountSync.swift:273-274`).\n- (b) Compare the login identity first: when local and remote hold the same `md_user`/`md_passs`, keep whichever cookie expiries are later, whatever the stamps say.\n\nTest (Linux, `HFRStorageTests`): two `AccountSync` over one item, B's clock one hour ahead. A's refresh after B's write must leave A's later expiries in A's store, and a re-login on A must win.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_\n",
